# Web-app typography

A Home Assistant dashboard looks the same in a web browser and in the Companion app, because both show it with the Civica theme. Set its text once, with the [typeface](typography-typeface.html) and [type scale](typography-type-scale.html), and it works in both.

Most text on a dashboard is readings, names and states that people check many times a day. Set them so the number, its unit and how recent it is can be read at a glance.

<h2 id="the-phones-own-screens">The phone's own screens</h2>

Do not restyle text that the phone draws itself. In the Companion app, the app's own settings screens, the keyboard, the share sheet and permission requests use the phone's own font. Leave them as they are. They match the rest of the phone, and people already know how to use them.

<h2 id="bg-typ-03">Keep readings, units and times together</h2>

Show a reading with its unit and the time it was taken. For example: “Living room 21°C, updated 14:05”. Keep the text selectable, so people can copy a reading.

| Do | Do not |
| --- | --- |
| −0.16 kW | 0.16 kW, when the house is exporting |
| 21.5°C | 21°C, when the half degree matters |
| Unavailable | 0°C, or a blank space |
| 18.2°C, updated 09:10 | 18.2°C, with no time, from a sensor that stopped reporting yesterday |

Never cut off the minus sign, the decimal part, the unit, the device name or a word such as “Unavailable” or “stale”. Line up digits in a column with [tabular numbers](typography-overrides.html#what-you-can-change).

Keep the language, number format, time format and time zone the household chose in Home Assistant. [Charts and data](../content/formatting-content/charts-and-data.html) explains how to word numbers and times.

Do not put a changing reading inside an image. It cannot update, and a screen reader cannot read it.

