<h2 id="bg-typ-04">Keep native screens dense</h2>

Native Home Assistant screens, such as entity settings and the automation editor, show a lot of information. They keep the smaller [native sizes](typography-type-scale.html#native-home-assistant-sizes). Do not make every editor row the size of a heading. That pushes controls off the screen and breaks the native layout.

Chart labels and legends in Civica chart cards are at least 14px, and grow with the font size scale.

These are Civica settings for its own theme and cards. A card from another developer may not follow them.

<h2 id="bg-typ-05">Check text at larger sizes and in difficult states</h2>

Check every card with browser zoom and with a raised font size scale. Labels, errors and help text must get taller, not be cut off. Test with:

- a long room name, such as “Upstairs landing by the airing cupboard”
- a large reading, such as 1,284.6 kWh
- a reading with no unit, an Unavailable state and an empty list
- a Welsh or other translated label, which is often longer than the English

A tooltip only adds detail. Keyboard and touch users need the name and state without hovering. If a compact native control cuts off text that is not essential, keep the full name available to screen readers. Give a way to see it by keyboard and touch. Do not turn off zoom or fix a card's height to hide text that does not fit.

[Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) gives the full zoom and reflow checks.
